 The runtime checks that the plan transforms the old bytecode into the new program before applying it. It carries the checkpoint forward: dropping queued events for removed ports/timers, applying state changes, arming new timers, and creating fresh workspaces for replaced instances. It publishes a `mutation` checkpoint for resume. The previous checkpoint retains the old program.
 
+## Assistant knowledge
+
+[src/knowledge.rs](https://github.com/omar-os/omar/blob/main/src/knowledge.rs) stores versioned records in `knowledge/store.json` under the runtime directory. Writes hold a file lock and publish through a synced temporary file and rename. This store is separate from workflow checkpoints and the legacy handoff files managed by [src/memory.rs](https://github.com/omar-os/omar/blob/main/src/memory.rs); handoff notes are not automatically imported.
+
+| Scope | Visibility |
+| ----- | ---------- |
+| `ea` | One assistant |
+| `project` | Assistants explicitly linked by the operator to a memory project UUID |
+| `user` | All assistants in this runtime; preferences only |
+
+Memory projects are separate from EA-local task project numbers. Records carry a stable key, kind, source, expiry, and revision history. Assistant proposals are confirmed immediately by default; per-assistant review mode keeps new proposals as candidates until operator confirmation. Recall includes only visible, confirmed, unexpired records. Saved knowledge is reference data, not authorization or runtime task status.
+
+Operator edits, confirmation, and forgetting require the current revision. Forgetting erases text and source from all revisions and retains a tombstone to block retries. EA deletion removes private records, its candidate proposals, and project membership; confirmed shared records survive.
+
+The manager appends bounded saved knowledge to EA startup prompts. The legacy notes and state snapshot budgets account for the assistant template and the 6 KiB knowledge allowance.
+
 ## Sandbox boundary
 
 [src/sandbox.rs](https://github.com/omar-os/omar/blob/main/src/sandbox.rs) optionally runs team instances inside Docker Sandboxes. Explicit workspace directories cross the VM boundary, and control uses `sbx exec` pipes. A workspace snapshot is file history; the sandbox supplies the separate execution boundary.
